so what is the origin of the law well we  don't know for certain but we think it  comes out of mesopotamia no not  hammurabi's code that dates from the  18th century bce specifically the city  state of ur and then ruler ur namu  around the year 2100 bce now there are  some earlier references to a set of laws  from urukagina in the 24th century bce  but the ernamu code is the oldest extant  set of laws that we still have it's  written in a set of if-then statements  if someone does this then this happens  so not surprisingly if a man commits  murder he shall be killed if a man  commits robbery he shall be killed but  what's particularly interesting about  this code is that unlike the later  babylonian codes like hammurabi which  purely focus on retribution an eye for  an eye this creates a set of monetary  compensation for certain acts so for  example if you cut off a man's foot you  owe that person 10 shekels of silver if  you knock out someone's tooth you owe  them two shekels of silver so this is  fairly advanced stuff in part of the  sumerian renaissance of the time and  that is as far as we can tell the origin  of the law
 

